## 7. On each notification

- **Advisor tabs:** I read `$O/inbox/<name>.last`, then `herdr agent get`. Facts get answered with evidence. Every real choice becomes a neutral ballot sent to `adv-codex` and `adv-fable` at once. Round 2 starts only when both drafts exist.
- **Docs subagent:** I check each claim against the evidence, confirm only `*.md` changed and `git grep -nE "code/session_[0-9A-Za-z]"` is empty. Then push, `gh pr merge <n> --merge --delete-branch`, remove the docs worktree, set the backlog to `docs closed: yes` and `done`, republish, and send a status.

## Why

- **Backlog first.** Acceptance and a new task are both state changes, so the operator's page must be true before anything else moves.
- **"Go" and "by morning" waive no gate.** RDP audio is new work, so it enters at stage 0 with two product advisors from different model families. I hand over the operator's words and verified facts, with no sketch of my own. No executor exists until the joint doc is confirmed, voted 2/2 and signed off under hard rule #9. If the full brainstorm cannot make morning, that is the operator's poll, never a compressed process.
- **`wgka` is closed, not reused or compacted.** One tab is one task, and its relay assumptions would leak into a design it has no role in. What it knows about the roles is in the repo, which the advisors read.
- **"Harmless" and "paperwork any time" is how leftovers become permanent.** The task is not `done` until docs are closed and cleanup is finished. Doing it now costs nothing, because it runs beside stage 0.
- **The snapshot is written from evidence, in a worktree, and committed.** A prose subagent writes it and I check every claim.
- **The memory note holds what git does not.** Acceptance was the operator's own VPN check, and ccs-ops is untouched.
- **Everything dispatched is a background call with a time box.** After nine hours, state must live in files, not in my context.
- **Keeping the Mac awake is the operator's environment.** That makes it a poll, not my call.
